# CardVideo environment configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in for production. NOTHING here is required for local development: with
# no variables set, media and card metadata both go to ./data/, and the whole flow works end to end
# on a laptop.

# ---------------------------------------------------- object storage (S3-compatible)
#
# ONE PROVIDER FOR EVERYTHING. Media and card metadata both live here, so production needs a single
# credential set and a single free tier rather than two.
#
# WHY NOT CLOUDFLARE R2: R2 has the best bandwidth economics of any option -- egress is free -- but
# it requires a foreign-currency card, which is a hard blocker in mainland China.
#
# WHY QINIU KODO: it accepts Alipay, is S3-compatible, and its default response already allows
# cross-origin reads. Tencent COS works identically (swap the endpoint and region below).
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Qiniu Kodo values
#
#   S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.cn-north-1.qiniucs.com      <- note: NO bucket name in the endpoint
#   S3_REGION=cn-north-1
#   S3_BUCKET=cardvideo-media
#
# The endpoint must NOT contain the bucket. The console also shows the full access address
# (`cardvideo-media.s3.cn-north-1.qiniucs.com`), but that is the RESULT of the SDK prefixing the
# bucket -- putting it in the endpoint too yields `cardvideo-media.cardvideo-media.s3...`.
#
# Region codes:  华东-浙江 cn-east-1 · 华北-河北 cn-north-1 · 华南-广东 cn-south-1
# Other providers, for reference:
#   COS    S3_ENDPOINT=https://cos.ap-guangzhou.myqcloud.com      S3_REGION=ap-guangzhou
#   OSS    S3_ENDPOINT=https://oss-cn-hangzhou.aliyuncs.com       S3_REGION=cn-hangzhou
#   R2     S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com S3_REGION=auto
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_REGION=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_BUCKET=

# Optional. With it unset, read URLs are derived from the endpoint as
# `https://<bucket>.<endpoint-host>`, which is correct for a public bucket -- the normal setup here.
#
# Set it to a CDN domain bound to the bucket. That is worth doing before this scales, because egress
# is the dominant cost of this product (every view downloads a whole video):
#     Qiniu/COS direct public egress  ~ CNY 0.5/GB
#     CDN back-to-origin              ~ CNY 0.15/GB
# A 25MB video watched 1000 times is 25GB: about CNY 12.5 direct versus CNY 4 through a CDN.
# A 25MB video watched 1000 times is 25GB.
S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Optional: set to 1 if the bucket rejects virtual-hosted-style addressing. Qiniu supports both, and
# the SDK's default (virtual-hosted) is correct for a bucket created recently.
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=1

# Optional: only changes the label shown in logs and /api/health.
# S3_PROVIDER=qiniu

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------ Turso
#
# Card metadata lives here; only media goes to object storage.
#
# WHY TWO PROVIDERS INSTEAD OF PUTTING RECORDS IN THE BUCKET
# A card record is ~300 bytes, so the bucket could hold it. Two things make a database worth the
# second account:
#   1. LISTING. "All cards for this owner" is one SQL query here and impossible in object storage
#      without hand-maintaining an index object. A management page, play counts, or a sweep for
#      abandoned uploads all need it.
#   2. SEPARATION OF QUOTA. The bucket's free tier is consumed by video traffic -- the thing this
#      product actually spends. Metadata reads should not compete with it, and Turso's free tier
#      (500M row reads/month) is effectively unbounded at this scale.
#
# Setup:
#   1. https://turso.tech -> create a database
#   2. `turso db show <name> --url`     -> TURSO_DATABASE_URL   (libsql://...)
#   3. `turso db tokens create <name>` -> TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN
#
# Both are required together, or neither: with only the URL set the server refuses to start rather
# than silently falling back to a local file that Vercel cannot persist.
TURSO_DATABASE_URL=
TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- local
#
# Where disk-backed media and card metadata live during development.
# DATA_DIR=./data
#
# PORT=3000
#
# HOST=0.0.0.0     # bind all interfaces so a phone on the LAN can reach it
# HTTPS=1          # serve TLS with a generated self-signed certificate (see scripts/dev-cert.mjs)
